@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_008.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Your Turn: Recognizing Homologs

@body

Two chromosome 14 homologs appear in a karyotype. What should they have in common?

- A. Similar length
- B. Same centromere position
- C. Same banding pattern
- D. The same genes at corresponding locations
- E. Always an X shape, even before DNA replication

=== layout: one-panel

# Recognizing Homologs: Answer

@body

- [*A, B, C, and D*]{color=#007A00}
- Homologs carry the same genes; their alleles can differ
- The X shape comes from *DNA replication*
- Each replicated homolog has two sister chromatids
- A homolog is still the same chromosome before replication

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Painting

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosome Painting

@body

- Fluorescent DNA probes bind to matching chromosome sequences
- *Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH)* makes those sequences visible
- A mixture of probes can give each chromosome type a different color

=== layout: one-panel

# *Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization (FISH)*

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_010.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Multicolor FISH and SKY

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_011.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *How Does SKY Work?*

@body

- Large collection of probes. Each probe is complementary to a unique region of one chromosome &#8211; together the "library" is complementary to all of the chromosomes within the human genome.
- Each probe is labeled with a fluorescent color that is designated for a specific chromosome.
  - For example, probes that are complementary to chromosome 1 are labeled with yellow molecules, while those that are complementary to chromosome 2 are labeled with red molecules, and so on.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Comparison*

@body

- There are two slightly different imaging systems that utilize the polarized fluorescence filters to distinguish a mixture of chromosomal paints in a karyotype.
  - multicolor FISH (mFISH; Speicher et al., 1996).
  - spectral karyotyping (SKY; Schrock et al., 1996)
- Main difference\:
  - one image acquisition with post-analysis in SKY versus sequential imaging with specific filters in mFISH.

