# Other Chromosome Shapes

@left

- [*Acentric*]{color=#D40000}: no centromere; usually lost during cell division
- [*Dicentric*]{color=#076DAD}: two centromeres; may be unstable if pulled toward opposite poles

@top-right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_026.png)

@bottom-right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_027.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Your Turn: Chromosome Shapes

@body

Match each description to a term.

| Description | Your Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Centromere near the middle; nearly equal arms | ? |
| Centromere slightly off-center | ? |
| Very short p arm and long q arm | ? |
| No centromere | ? |
| Two centromeres | ? |

*Metacentric, submetacentric, acrocentric, acentric, dicentric*

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosome Shapes: Answer

@body

| Description | Term |
| --- | --- |
| Nearly equal arms | [*Metacentric*]{color=#0067CC} |
| Slightly unequal arms | [*Submetacentric*]{color=#C80085} |
| Very short p arm | [*Acrocentric*]{color=#007A00} |
| No centromere | [*Acentric*]{color=#D40000} |
| Two centromeres | [*Dicentric*]{color=#076DAD} |

=== layout: one-panel

# *Number of Chromosomes*

@body

- Mosquito\: 6
- Drosophila (fruit fly)\: 8
- Onion\: 16
- Cat, Pig\: 38
- Dolphin, Rabbit\: 44
- Humans\: 46
- Tobacco, Monkeys, Great Apes\: 48
- Dog, Chicken\: 78
- Carp\: 104

