=== layout: section

# *The Story of* *HeLa Cells*

=== layout: one-panel

# HeLa Cells

@body

- An immortal human cell line used in research
- Cervical cancer cells taken from *Henrietta Lacks* in 1951
- *He* from Henrietta + *La* from Lacks
- She died of her cancer on October 4, 1951
- Durable and fast-growing; HeLa cells have also contaminated other cell lines

=== layout: two-panels

# Cause of Ms. Lacks' Cancer

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_033.png)

@right

- Persistent infection with high-risk *HPV* causes almost all cervical cancers
- Types *16 and 18* account for about 70% worldwide
- Most HPV infections clear without causing cancer
- Vaccination helps prevent infection with cancer-causing types

@citation

https://www.cancer.gov/types/cervical/causes-risk-prevention

=== layout: two-panels

# Why HeLa Cells Matter

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_034.png)

@right

- Human cells that could keep growing in the laboratory
- Cells for repeated experiments
- Helped develop the polio vaccine
- Used in cancer and HIV research, drug testing, radiation studies, and gene mapping

=== layout: two-panels

# Henrietta Lacks

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_035.png)

@right

- An African-American patient whose tumor cells were taken in 1951 without her knowledge or consent
- Her family learned about the cells decades later, when researchers contacted them for blood samples
- Her story raises questions about consent, privacy, and who benefits from research

=== layout: one-panel

# *HeLa Karyotype Is Crazy!!!*

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10b-karyotypes/image_036.png)

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Karyotypes arrange condensed chromosomes in homologous pairs.
- Banding and fluorescent probes help identify chromosomes.
- Centromere position distinguishes chromosome shapes.
- HeLa cells connect chromosome variation, research, and consent.

